    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "When a fiber optic cable is physically disturbed, the light passing through it experiences a small phase shift.  The magnitude of this phase shift is proportional to that of the disturbance, allowing us to reconstruct the original vibration signal based on the light we observe.  By employing detectors at both ends of the cable, we can moreover determine precisely where the disturbance occurred.  Here we describe advances in the techniques of localizing these disturbances, ultimately with an eye for earthquake detection in the middle of the oceans, where there is a deep poverty of seismological data.  \r\n\r\nIn particular, we present a novel time delay estimation technique which enables fast, precise results even in systems with common noise.  Traditional correlation techniques fail in such settings, with the noise difficult to distinguish from the signal.  We instead appeal to a geometric relationship between the difference of two signals with small time delay and their derivative, enabling us to outperform existing techniques by at least an order of magnitude.  We also gain the ability to localize multiple simultaneous signals, which is not possible with correlation-based techniques even in principle.  These advances open up long-distance fiber optic seismometry as a viable technology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Brandon, J. (2024). Derivative Matching: A Novel Time Delay Estimation Technique for Localization of Signals in Undersea Fiber Optic Seismometry. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hd270tn0985. https://doi.org/10.25740/hd270tn0985.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
